WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:14.198
This is Express, the web framework from Node.js, running on Osprey with no Node.js at all. We'll start with the measurement: the same Express application, served by Osprey and by real Express, under the same load.

00:14.948 --> 00:31.159
This is the comparison bench from the Express package. It takes three example applications and serves each one three ways: compiled by Osprey with the garbage collector, compiled by Osprey with reference counting, and run by real Express on Node.js.

00:31.359 --> 00:46.868
Each server gets the same requests over loopback, sixteen in flight at once: a thousand to warm up, then ten thousand that are timed. That is repeated for five trials, and the servers take turns, so a busy moment on the machine hits each of them alike.

00:47.068 --> 00:52.770
Every timed reply is checked against what Express answered, so a fast wrong answer does not count.

00:52.970 --> 01:13.039
And here is the table. Read the reference counting rows against the Node rows. Requests per second are within three percent of each other on every example, and neither side leads on all three. The last column is peak memory: three megabytes for Osprey with reference counting, and between a hundred and thirty and a hundred and fifty for Node.

01:13.239 --> 01:28.113
The garbage collected build is the slowest of the three, and on the web service example it uses the most memory. So this is not a claim that Osprey beats Express on speed. It is one machine, three small examples, and the numbers as they came out.

02:14.815 --> 02:20.917
Here's what is being measured. The first example is the application from the Express README, unchanged.

02:21.117 --> 02:24.250
Osprey compiles it ahead of time to a native binary.

02:24.450 --> 02:28.614
There is no Node.js in that binary, and no JavaScript engine.

02:28.814 --> 02:33.155
And every reply is compared with what real Express sends for the same file.

02:33.905 --> 02:37.812
This is the file. It imports express and creates an application.

02:38.012 --> 02:43.025
It registers one route. A GET request for the root path is answered with Hello World.

02:43.225 --> 02:47.648
Then it listens on port three thousand, and logs a line once the server is up.

02:47.848 --> 03:03.215
Now run it with Osprey. The compiler reads the TypeScript, checks the types, and warns that the request parameter is never used. Then it builds a native program and starts it. The line from the callback prints, and the process stays up, as it would under Node.

03:13.205 --> 03:23.651
In the second terminal, request the root path. The reply carries the status, the X-Powered-By header, the content type Express chooses for a string, and the body: Hello World.

03:23.851 --> 03:39.805
Now ask for a path that no route matches. Express has a final handler for that, and the port has it too. The server's log on the left records a four oh four, and the reply carries the security headers and the same small page, which says it cannot GET that path.

03:51.405 --> 04:01.093
How does the port work? It is written in the same TypeScript flavor. Calling express returns an application, which is a list of routes and a list of error handlers.

04:01.293 --> 04:08.548
Get takes the application, a path and a handler, and returns a new application with one more route. Nothing is mutated.

04:08.748 --> 04:12.920
With route appends a record of the method, the path and the handler to the list.

04:13.670 --> 04:22.368
The response is a record too. It holds the status, the body, the content type and the headers, and a signal that tells the dispatcher what to do with it.

04:22.568 --> 04:33.155
Status, set, send and json each return a new response with one thing changed. That is how a handler written as statements, or as a chain, builds the same reply.

04:33.905 --> 04:37.856
Listen starts the native HTTP listener with the application.

04:38.056 --> 04:41.898
If it started, the callback runs and the caller gets a listening server.

04:42.098 --> 04:51.881
If the port could not be bound, that is a value as well: the error is printed and returned. Osprey has no exceptions, so the failure cannot be skipped by accident.

04:52.631 --> 05:08.923
The third example is Express's own web service example, ported. This middleware is mounted on slash A P I. It reads the A P I key from the query string. With no key, it fails the request with a four hundred, and an error handler turns that into JSON.

05:09.123 --> 05:16.294
Run it, and ask for the users with a valid key. The middleware passes the request on, and the route answers with JSON.

05:16.494 --> 05:29.129
Ask again without a key. The reply is four hundred, Bad Request, with the error as JSON. In the tests, every one of these replies is compared with the original example running on real Express.

05:51.831 --> 05:58.929
Back to the bench for a moment, because the numbers only mean something with the method. Sixteen requests are in flight at a time.

05:59.129 --> 06:02.628
A thousand warm up each server, and ten thousand are timed.

06:02.828 --> 06:09.202
And each server runs five trials. The bench is one file in the package, so you can run it on your own machine.

06:09.952 --> 06:15.687
Now change it. In the README example, select the word World, and type Osprey. Save the file.

06:15.887 --> 06:23.823
Run it again. Osprey compiles the edited file and serves it. Request the root path, and the reply is the new text: Hello Osprey.

06:38.919 --> 06:43.310
Some limits. The port is in progress, and this film shows only what exists.

06:43.510 --> 06:46.891
There is no Router yet, and no JSON request bodies.

06:47.091 --> 06:52.310
Handlers do not yet run in parallel across cores, which is the next thing to fix for throughput.

06:52.510 --> 06:56.266
And under the garbage collector, the port is slower than Express today.

06:57.016 --> 07:05.259
To try it, serve the README example, request it with curl, and run the comparison bench yourself. The port's source is one file. Read it.
